go to the supermarket and there are  always offers to be had but many of  those products are high in fat sugar and  salt and come the autumn multi-deals on  such items were due to be banned  but now the government has backtracked  saying it wants to prevent a further  squeeze on living standards  that hasn't gone down well with health  campaigners like graham mcgregor a  professor in cardiovascular medicine  since 1980 we reckon they've been  approximately 30 plans from the uk to  try and prevent obesity  not one of them has been implemented and  what's happening is just getting more  and more obese  and it's a real disaster in public  health and it's really sad that boris  johnson has decided not to implement was  flagship policies he thinks the prime  minister has buckled under pressure from  some of his party's mps what he calls an  aggressive food industry and he's not  alone  boris wants to stay in power boris will  do anything to be in power he should  never have been in power  he never lied about who he was he lied  about what his policies were i don't  think they're helping people because if  health gets worse and it puts more  pressure on the nhs then it's going to  cost people more money in the long run  anyway i mean it is it's a good idea  isn't it because everyone's trying to  survive out here really hope it's been a  problem so yeah i mean to delay it it's  a good thing isn't it i'd say but  banning it i don't understand why  they've banned it in the first place in  theory the ban will be enforced 12  months later in october 2023 and an end  to junk food adverts on tv before 9 pm  will eventually happen in 2024 to  prevent children being targeted with  unhealthy treats  interestingly when you go into some of  the big supermarkets there's barely a  multi-buyer on offer it doesn't mean to  say there aren't deals though so this is  a shop of 10 items fruit veg meat fish  cakes biscuits cereal  it should have cost 22 pounds instead it  costs less than 16.  it could be a sign that stores were  already making changes to the way they  offer discounts knowing the bog off or  buy one get one free was soon to be a  distant memory  the government says the delay will  provide crucial time to understand the  impact the ban would have on shoppers  struggling with the cost of living  crisis but critics argue instead it's  playing politics with children's health  emma birchley sky news west london
 

